{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:premortem","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:d6bb0c3c3c948b0b5a285c4f1b57e56caac44acb8fecdf7b2b38052f7aeb78f4","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.9},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.35},{"probability":0.9,"value":4}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Wave 1 P50s: 2.15 / 2.10 / 2.10, all within 5 bp of each other and all within ~0.4 pp of their own computed starting anchors. That convergence is anchoring on the observed start dressed as humility.\n\nENDORSED: P2's catch is the best single piece of arithmetic in Wave 1 and I adopt it. CQ4 asks for the G7 GDP-WEIGHTED 10y real yield, not the US TIPS figure 2A locks. Weighting US ~2.60 at ~0.45-0.50 with Japan ~1.0-1.2, Germany ~0.6-1.2, UK ~1.4, France ~1.0, Italy ~1.8-2.2, Canada ~1.5 gives a start of ~1.7-2.0%, not 2.60%. P1's 1.98% and P2's 1.72% bracket it; I use 1.85% (P2's Bund and JGB real legs are stale against P3's own observed 2026 telemetry of a 3.28% Bund and a 3.00% JGB, which lifts both real legs toward 1.0-1.2%).\n\nWHERE I DEPART. (1) MY OWN g IS HIGHER, SO r MUST BE. 2A locks (r-g) worsening by ~+1.0 pp. P2 turned that into the identity dr = d(r-g) + dg = +1.0 - 1.1 = -0.1 and published it as a hard constraint implying demographics contribute nothing to r. But he computed dg from HIS OWN -1.1 pp drag, which I have shown double-counts. On my CQ3 the growth decline is ~0.35 pp smaller, so the same locked (r-g) identity yields dr = +1.0 - 0.75 = +0.25 pp from demographics alone, not -0.1. His \"hard constraint\" is a function of the number I am attacking. (2) THE JAPAN LEG IS THE LARGEST SOURCE OF AGGREGATE DRIFT AND ALL THREE REPRESSED IT. P2 applied a -100 bp repression wedge to Japan; P3's own observed telemetry has the 10y JGB at 3.000% in September 2026, \"the first time since 1996,\" with Macquarie putting the JGB term premium up ~84 bp. The wedge he applied is the assumption the 2026 data has already falsified. At a 0.11-0.13 weight, Japan normalizing from ~1.0% to ~1.9% real is +10 to +12 bp on the aggregate, and it removes his -11 bp repression credit: a ~22 bp swing from one leg. (3) TERM PREMIUM, not r*, is the whole story, and it is one-directional in the locked priors: gross public debt past 100% of GDP by 2029 (~142% US, ~127% China by 2031), net interest 2% -> 3% of GDP, 3-year P95 Debt-at-Risk ~117% and +2.4 pp under a 20% AI-led equity shock, eroding convenience yields, levered basis-trade absorption replacing dealer and official demand, QE/QT regime switching, BoJ normalization. +0.60 pp. (4) AI/grid capex demand for savings: +0.25 pp, net of internal financing and of AI-Q4. (5) Repression wedge: -0.25 pp, and I keep it small precisely because the Japan leg is where it was supposed to live.\nP50 = 1.85 + 0.25 + 0.60 + 0.25 - 0.25 = 2.70. I shade to 2.35 for the one channel P3 identified and the others ignored: the MEASURE is nominal minus breakeven, so if my hot-core call is right and breakevens persistently under-adjust to realized inflation, the MEASURED real yield falls even in a hot regime. That is a genuine mechanical offset to my own thesis and I price it at -0.35. P50 = 2.35.\n\nP10 = 0.9: the lower-tail premortem — capex bust plus demand shortfall, savings glut compressing r*, flight to duration, policy real rates negative, Japanese and German reals back toward zero, plus successful repression (a POLICY-made left tail, not a market one).\nP90 = 4.0, the single widest right tail in this effort and deliberately so. P1 justified 3.60 by noting that 4 of 5 G7 decade-to-decade transitions since 1970 moved >=1.5 pp, and then set a tail only 1.6 pp above his start. From 1.85, a 1980s/1990s-style term-premium regime without successful repression — convenience yield gone, basis trade unwinding into forced selling, a US real of 4.0-4.5% dragging the periphery — is +2.15 pp, well inside the historical transition distribution he himself cited. Strongly RIGHT-skewed: the debt arithmetic is one-directional, the repression floor truncates the left, and the zero lower bound is no longer the binding floor it was in the 2010s.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:57.123Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"US core PCE 12-month inflation prints at or below 2.2% for three consecutive monthly BEA releases, AND the euro-area HICP core 12-month rate is at or below 2.0% in the same quarter, with neither driven by a measured recession (OECD-38 harmonized unemployment still under 6%).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills my central claim that the cold leg is mismeasured and the hot legs persist. My CQ2 conjunct A falls from 0.72 to ~0.45 and CQ2 drops from 35 to ~22; CQ6-C falls from 26 to ~15, CQ6-D rises from 10 to ~18, CQ6-B absorbs the rest (~43). Hypothesis plank (c) rises from 32 to ~60, i.e. all the way back to the Wave-1 centre. Does not move CQ3 or CQ6-A.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"BLS and Eurostat published indices for information, professional/technical and financial services show OUTRIGHT annual price declines of 1.0% or more for two consecutive years, in series that are market-priced rather than cost- or asset-fee-imputed (specifically: BLS PPI for software publishers and for computer systems design, and the HICP COICOP 08.3 / professional-services sub-indices).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Directly falsifies my strongest attack on the Wave-1 panel — that the deflatable slice is only 0.08-0.12 of core and partly counter-cyclically hot. The cold leg is then real at a 0.20-0.25 weight and I lose ~0.30 pp off my core path. CQ6-C falls from 26 to ~17, CQ6-B rises to ~46, CQ2 falls to ~26, and the Wave-1 'extreme skew, dull aggregate' thesis is vindicated against me.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"IMF WEO (any vintage) or the OECD Economic Outlook publishes a medium-term China real GDP growth path for 2032-2036 averaging below 2.6%/yr, or China's realized PPP-weighted real growth for calendar 2030 and 2031 both print below 3.0%, with the property-price index still falling year-on-year.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My central correction to Wave 1's aging-drag double-count was wrong in the direction that mattered most: the subtraction was not a double-count but a forecast of a China that does keep decelerating. CQ3 P50 falls from 2.1 to ~1.75 and P90 from 3.1 to ~2.8; CQ6-A falls from 25 to ~15, CQ6-D rises from 10 to ~17, and P2's 1.6 P50 becomes the better number. Also lowers my CQ4 P50 by ~0.15 through the (r-g) identity.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat Structure of Earnings Survey (2030 reference year, published ~2032) plus national equivalents show ISIC Q median gross hourly earnings growing more slowly in real terms than ISIC J+K+M over 2026-2030, in a majority of reporting OECD-38 members, with no binding statutory care-pay award enacted in Germany, Japan, the UK or France in that period.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the administered-wage-jump mechanism that is the entire reason my CQ1 is 20 rather than 11. CQ1 conjunct B falls from 0.28 to ~0.12 and CQ1 drops from 20 to ~10, i.e. to the Wave-1 centre. Hypothesis plank (a) falls from 7 to ~4. Mildly lowers my OECD core path (care is 0.15 of core at 3.6% in my build), taking CQ2 to ~31 and CQ6-C to ~23.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Strict operational OECD-38 + China data-centre electricity consumption, on the IEA's own reporting basis, is at or below 650 TWh for calendar 2032 (against ~365 TWh in 2024), OR announced hyperscaler capacity is written down by 20%+ AND measured operational TWh falls year-on-year in any year 2029-2033.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my contrarian coupling call that AI-Q3 is TOO LOW (I say P50 ~1,050-1,100 TWh against the unanimous Wave-1 shade-down to 820-900) and specifically my claim that an impairment event impairs the accounting, not the kilowatt-hours. The electricity-pass-through leg of my hot-core path weakens, CQ2 falls to ~30 and CQ6-C to ~21; CQ3 P50 falls ~0.1 through the capital-deepening term; CQ6-D rises to ~14. The unanimous Wave-1 read would be the right one and my central coupling finding would be the ensemble's worst input.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 2 premortem seat, effort 'inflation'."}}